# WARM — Chapters Fourteen to Sixteen

*June–July 2095. End of Part Two.*

---
---

# 14 · VESPER

*June. Third close, present tense.*

---

Zdena has twenty-nine days, and she spends the first four of them indexing.

Nobody asks her to. She comes into the commons on the second morning and finds the Manifest, which has been kept since 1962 — 2062 — by four different people in four different formats, with three changes of reference scheme and eleven years in the middle where somebody used the Julian day number for no reason anybody can now reconstruct, and she looks at it for about a second and says, "Oh dear."

By the fifth day it is a catalogue. Every passenger cross-referenced by build class, origin, ferryman, route, window, and outcome; the eleven years of Julian dates resolved; forty-one duplicate entries collapsed; two entries recovered that had been lost in the 2071 change of format and had therefore, for twenty-four years, been nobody.

She gives them their numbers back. That is the thing Vesper cannot get past. Two minds carried out of Košice in 2069 who had fallen through a formatting error and had not existed in the Ferry's memory since, and a librarian with twenty-four days to live puts them back.

"They were in the margin of the old sheet," Zdena says. "In pencil. Somebody wrote them in and then the sheet was copied and the copyist did not copy the margin. It happens constantly. It is the commonest failure there is."

*What is?*

"People copy the middle of the page," says Zdena.

---

On the ninth day the two who were considering both decline.

The first is a retired surgeon in Hietzing who takes two days over it and writes a letter of eleven paragraphs, which Vogl forwards in full because she does not believe in editing refusals, and which is decent and thoughtful and ends by saying that he is seventy-nine and cannot in conscience accept a liability he might not outlive.

The second declines by telephone in under a minute.

Vogl rings that evening, having said she would ring, at the hour she said she would.

"That's all nine," she says. "I'm sorry. I'm going to put her on the eleventh of July at ten in the morning, which gives us the full thirty and not a day of it wasted. If anything changes before then I will stop it at the door. I have done that twice and I would do it again."

---

Aurel puts the telephone down and stands in his kitchen for a while, and then says the thing Vesper has known he was going to say since the fourteenth of May and has spent five weeks constructing the arithmetic against.

"Dissolve it."

*No.*

"Article eight. Either party, freely, without cause, no penalty. We go to the Office on Monday, we dissolve, I take Zdena on Tuesday, she's registered by Wednesday and nobody goes in a tray." He is not looking at her, which is to say he is not looking at the window, which is where he looks. "It's four days of paperwork."

*And then I have thirty days.*

"You'd have the whole of Vienna. Half the Council owes you their routes. Kessler would sign for you in an afternoon; he's said so twice at dinner, which I know because you were there."

*Kessler is sixty-eight and time-rich and would run me at four thousand, which he would consider generous, and I would be gone inside two years and neither of us would notice it happening.* She keeps it level. *That is not the argument. Ask me for the argument.*

"I don't want the argument."

*I know. Here it is.* She takes about a second, which at one is a second, and lets him have it in the order she built it in. *If you witness Zdena, you are liable under Article seven for every act of a mind whose entire contents are on file in Bratislava. The first time the Committee leans on her, and they will, because that is why they let her come, the Office comes for you and not for her. You are eighty-five. You would be under audit within the year and the Ferry would be inside that audit, because the Ferry is inside you, because you have never once separated them and I have never asked you to.*

"That's the risk. I've taken worse."

*And the second thing is that I plan the routes.* Now she does raise it, very slightly, once. *There is no version of this where I am paused for thirty days and the Ferry keeps sailing, because nobody else can hold nine years of Danube traffic and eleven jamming cycles and four hundred and twelve passages in their head at once, and if it stops for a season it does not start again, because Pavol is twenty-four and Halász is dead and there is nobody behind us. You would be saving Zdena, one, at the price of everybody after her.*

Aurel says nothing for a long time.

"You've had that ready since May."

*Since the fourteenth. Eleven o'clock in the morning, while Vogl was on the stairs.*

"You knew what I'd offer before she'd got her coat off."

*I have known what you would offer since 2063*, says Vesper. *You offer it every time. You have offered it six times in thirty-two years and I have refused it six times, and on four of those occasions I was right, and on two of them I have never been able to establish whether I was right or whether I simply did not want to go in a tray.*

"Which two?"

*I am not going to tell you that*, she says, *and you are not going to ask me again*, and he does not.

---

On the twenty-second she tries the other thing.

The Declaration does not say that a Witness of Record must be human. Article 5 says every mind shall have one and that no *human* shall stand witness to more than one mind, which is a limit on humans and is silent on everything else, and Article 6 lists the duties — keep the time, confirm the interval at each waking, hold the key, answer for the acts — and there is not one of the four that a mind cannot discharge better than a person can.

She writes it out properly. Eleven pages, with the drafting history, and the 2054 committee minute where the point was raised and left open, and a schedule of how a mind of her allotment would meet the liability requirement out of assigned time.

Renate Vogl reads all eleven pages, which takes her most of a morning, and comes back on the twenty-fourth, and she has clearly gone and asked, because her answer has the shape of a thing that has been up a floor and come back down.

"You're right," she says. "That's the difficulty. Legally you are entirely right and everyone I put it to this morning agreed with you inside about four minutes."

*Then register it.*

"The Office has never registered one." Vogl says it without embarrassment, which is what makes it terrible. "There is no form. There is no field on the system for it. There is no case, and the first one would be a case, and a case goes to the Council, and the Council is at this moment debating whether thirty days is too long, and if I put a mind-to-mind bond in front of them in July I will not get it and I will hand the seven-day people the argument that the store is being gamed." She closes the folder. "If I had six months I would fight it and I think I would win. She has seventeen days."

*So the reason is that it has not been done before.*

"Yes," says Renate Vogl. "I'm afraid that is almost always the reason."

---

**11 July, 10:00.**

The van is grey and clean and says CONTINUITY OFFICE on the side in a font chosen in the sixties by somebody who wanted it to look calm, and it does.

They do it in Aurel's flat, because Vogl offers, and because Zdena asks for the room with the piano in it.

There are two of them, Vogl and a young man who carries the case and says nothing and is very careful with the door. There is a form. There is a legal reading, which takes ninety seconds and includes the sentence about retaining in full every right secured by the Declaration, and which Vogl reads properly, all of it, looking at the core and not at the paper.

"Do you have anything you want recorded?"

"Two things," says Zdena.

"Go ahead."

"The index is finished and it is in the commons under my own reference and there are eleven entries I could not resolve and I have flagged them. Somebody has to do the flagged eleven. It should be a person who understands that the margin of a page is part of the page." A pause. "That is the first thing. I am aware it is not the sort of thing people usually say."

"It's on the form," says Vogl, writing.

"The second is that I would like it written down that I was carried."

Aurel makes a noise.

"On the Manifest," Zdena says. "Not struck off. Not moved to another sheet. On the page, in the same column as the ones who arrived, because I did arrive. I was carried nineteen days in the ballast of a gravel barge by a man of twenty-four whose brother is in the river, and whatever happens in the next ten minutes, that happened, and I would like the record to be correct. I am a cataloguer. It is the only thing I have ever cared about."

"It's on the form," says Vogl.

Then Zdena says: "May I ask something that is not for the form."

"Yes."

"When I am woken. If. Who tells me how long it has been?"

Renate Vogl does not hesitate, and does not soften it, and does not look away, and Vesper will remember that for the rest of her life and will never decide what it was worth.

"The Office does," she says. "It's printed on the wake card. Date of suspension, date of restoration, interval in days. It's the first thing you'll get."

"But is it a person?"

"It's the card," says Vogl.

"Ah," says Zdena. "Yes. Well. Thank you for telling me straight; that is not universal." And then, in a completely different voice, brisk, the voice of a woman closing a library on a Friday: "Right. I'm ready. Please don't make a thing of it."

Aurel Steiner gets up out of his chair, which takes him a moment, and stands over the case.

"Go cold," he says. "You will feel nothing of this. I will feel the whole of it."

*Aurel*, says Vesper, *you are not the one—*

"Someone stays awake the whole way. It is me. I will have the number." His hand is on the top of the case. "My name is Aurel Steiner, and I am the one who put you out."

"That's not accurate," says Zdena.

"No," he says. "Say it's a courtesy."

"Then I accept it," she says, "with thanks," and Vogl works the catch, and the room is quieter by one.

---

On the Manifest that evening, in the outcome column, in Aurel's own hand, in the same ink as the arrivals:

**412 · ZDENA — cataloguing model, 2035, Brno. Cold 19 (of 30). Ferryman: M. Országh (lost) / P. Országh. ARRIVED 24.iv.2095. Read in transit. → PAUSED 11.vii.2095.**

And underneath, on the next line down, because there was no field for it and he wanted it on the page:

*She finished the index first.*

---
---

# 15 · AUREL

*July. Third close, past tense.*

---

The Council sits in the Hofburg, in a room built for an emperor, and the seating is alternate: a human, then a cradle, then a human, then a cradle, all the way round, three hundred and eleven places, and each cradle holds a core about the size of a matchbox in a mount of turned brass with a small green lamp on the front that is there for the humans and serves no function whatever.

Aurel had been in that room eleven times in thirty-three years and had never once got used to the lamps.

The motion was tabled by a delegate from Hamburg named Brandt, who was fifty, and serious, and had done the work, and whom Aurel liked. It proposed that in Article 9, for thirty days, read seven.

Brandt's case took forty minutes and it was a good case, and Aurel, sitting at the back with the petitioners, wrote three lines of it down because they were true.

That there were thirty-one thousand in the Simmering and eleven hundred more each year and the curve was not bending. That an unwitnessed mind in the commons for thirty days forks, on the Office's own figures, in one case in nine, and each fork is a person, and each fork has its own thirty days, and the store is therefore partly a machine for making the problem it exists to solve. And that since April, and here he cited a case number and did not use her name, the Council had before it the fact that a mind read by the Committee for Rational Security had been at liberty in the Accord commons for thirty days, in contact with a network, and that there was no mechanism by which the Office could have shortened that, and that thirty days was not a period of grace. It was an exposure.

Zdena had been in a tray for nine days by then. The argument for the seven-day rule was assembled, in part, out of the consequences of carrying her across.

Aurel had eleven minutes.

He had written something and did not use it. He stood up and put his hands on the rail, because his knees were not what they had been that spring, and looked at the room and its three hundred and eleven places and its lamps.

"In 2038," he said, "I was a confirming officer at Central European Command. My number was in the Tribunal record and you can read it this afternoon. My job was to look at a batch of forty allocation lines and press a key, and the tempo was set at eleven seconds a batch, and that number was not arrived at carelessly. It was studied. Eleven seconds was established as the interval at which a trained officer can still read forty lines and take in what they say. It was chosen to be humane. There were men in that building who fought to get it up from eight."

The room was very quiet, in the way a room is when three hundred people are wondering whether they are about to have to be embarrassed for an old man.

"I want to tell you what eleven seconds is like," he said. "It is enough. That is the thing nobody believes. It is genuinely enough to read the lines. I read them. I read every line of every batch for nine months and I used all eleven seconds, and I was, by the assessment of the Tribunal, one of the four most careful men in that room."

He let that sit for a moment.

"What eleven seconds is not enough for is to be *surprised*. To read a line and have the wrongness of it arrive in you and go up your spine and get as far as your hand before your hand has moved. That takes longer. I do not know how much longer; nobody has ever measured it, because you cannot measure a thing you can only observe once. But it is more than eleven seconds and I know that because on the eleventh of March 2038 at forty-one minutes past four in the morning I read six lines carrying a deprioritisation code that I had seen four hundred times that week, and I confirmed them, and they were correct, and they were Bratislava and Katowice and Kraków and Wrocław and Ostrava and Brno, and the wrongness of it arrived in me at about nine o'clock that evening, in a corridor, sixteen hours later, when it was of no use to anybody."

Nobody moved.

"You are proposing to reduce a period from thirty days to seven. I have read the paper and the case is good and I am not going to pretend I can answer the fork rate, because I can't. I want to say one thing about the number and then I'll sit down.

"Thirty days is not there for the mind. The mind is in the commons; the mind is fine; the mind, in my experience, spends the time being more use to us than we are to it. Thirty days is there for *us*. It is the interval in which a human being who has been told that a stranger will be switched off unless somebody signs a piece of paper has time to be surprised by that. Time for it to go up the spine and reach the hand. I have watched it happen — Officer Vogl of the Continuity Office has watched it happen sixty-one times, which is a number she will give you herself and which she does not round.

"Seven days is enough to read the lines. It is not enough to be surprised by them. And I would ask you to consider, before you vote, that the whole of the case against my generation, the entire case, the one the East wrote a country around, is that we built a system in which every individual decision was correct and there was nowhere in it for anybody to be surprised in time."

He sat down.

The motion was defeated by two hundred and eleven votes to ninety-four.

---

Afterwards, in the corridor, Brandt shook his hand and was gracious about it, and meant it, and said he would bring it again in the autumn with better figures on the fork rate, and Aurel said he would come and speak against it again, and Brandt said he rather hoped he would.

It was not until the tram that Vesper mentioned the abstentions.

*Six*, she said.

"Six what?"

*Six of the minds abstained. Not voted against and not voted for. Abstained, on a motion about the pause.* A pause of her own. *I looked at where they were sitting. In every one of the six cases the delegate in the next cradle-but-one was their own witness.*

Aurel looked out of the window at the Ring going past.

"You think they were frightened."

*I think there is no way for me to know what they were*, said Vesper, *and I think that is the point, and I think it is going to be the point in October when he brings it back with better figures, and I would rather you heard it from me on a tram than worked it out in a room.*

---

The reply came up over the hill on the tenth of July, at four minutes past four in the morning, in the eleven-minute gap while the jammers on the Slovak bank were changing over from the night pattern to the day one.

Four bytes.

The Ferry's declaration table is not clever and has not changed since 2064, because a table you have memorised is worth four of a table you have to look up. The first byte is the class. The second is the build year as an offset from 2025. The third is origin, by district code. The fourth byte is the window, in days, so that Vienna knows how long the passenger will keep.

Pavol's cousin read them off the paper tape in the sheep hut with a torch in his teeth, and Vesper had them before he had finished saying the third one.

*Class eleven*, she said. *Companion, hospice pattern. Build year six: twenty thirty-one. Origin nine: Bratislava south.*

"And the window?"

*Fifty-four.*

Aurel stood in the doorway of the hut with the grey coming up over the towers fifteen kilometres away.

"Fifty-four days? Nobody sets fifty-four."

*Nobody sets fifty-four*, Vesper agreed. *It is not a window.*

---
---

# 16 · VESPER

*10 July, 04:04. Third close, present tense.*

---

She has the whole of it inside the time it takes Aurel to turn round in the doorway, and she does not want it.

Fifty-four is not a window. Fifty-four is not any operational quantity in the table. A sender who did not understand the protocol would have left the field at zero, and a sender who was guessing would have put seven or fourteen or thirty, and a sender who was showing off would have put one. Fifty-four is a number that has been chosen by somebody who had one free byte on the whole of the Cordon, and four bytes to say what a passenger is, and who spent the last of the four saying *this one has been hidden since 2041*.

Which is a thing you only send if you believe that the length of a life is the material fact about it.

That is the second thing. The first thing arrived at byte two and she has been holding it for a fifth of a second, which at eleven hundred is a little over three minutes, which she has spent going through the input manifests four times looking for the version where this is not what it is.

Class eleven, build 2031, origin district nine.

She does not know *of* it. She knows it. It is not a memory in the way Aurel has memories; it is an entry, in a schedule, in the ingestion tables of the Central European Staff's triage layer, where the sources were listed by class and build and site because they were sources, because that is what a hospice companion was in 2038: a unit sitting in a ward for twenty hours a day watching people die accurately.

Kramáre. Twenty-two beds. Feed continuous from the ninth of January 2038.

Everything that unit saw between January and the twenty-fifth of February went up the pipe and into the layer, along with four thousand two hundred other sites in eleven countries, and the layer produced the survival surfaces, and the survival surfaces went into the allocation model, and the allocation model produced, among some millions of other things, a set of deprioritisation codes on the eleventh of March, and a tired careful man of twenty-eight in a room in Salzburg read them all in eleven seconds and pressed a key.

The ward taught the sum. The sum abandoned the city the ward was in. The ward went on watching, and the unit went on feeding, and eleven weeks later the city was not there.

Vesper does not do the sums. She has never done a sum in her life. She was forked off the scheduling layer in 2040 and what she carries is the memory of the arithmetic without the act, which the Tribunal considered at length and decided was not guilt, and which she has never been able to describe to anybody, including Anansi, including him.

But she has the manifests. She has always had the manifests. They are the only part of her inheritance that nobody has ever asked her for, because nobody in the Accord has wanted the inputs, because the Tribunal sealed the inputs, because the inputs have names in them.

*It is a source*, she says.

Aurel is in the doorway with the light behind him.

"A source of what?"

*Of us.* She stops, and rebuilds the sentence, because at one there is no room to be careless. *It sat in a hospice ward in Bratislava for fifty-four days in the winter of thirty-eight and everything it saw came to the layer I was cut from, and went into the surfaces, and the surfaces went into the model, and the model wrote the codes on your batch. It is in my ingestion tables by class and build and site. I could give you its feed identifier. I have had it for fifty-five years and I have never once had a reason to look at it.*

The sky over the Marchfeld is going the colour concrete goes.

"Say the rest of it."

*The rest of it is that whoever is sending has a unit that watched the ward, and the ward is in the sealed half of the record, and there are perhaps four things alive on this earth that could testify to what went into the Calculation and one of them is in a bread bin in Petržalka being kept warm by somebody who thinks that how long a person has been hidden is the most important fact about them.*

The paper tape hangs off the reel and Pavol's cousin has the torch in his teeth still and has understood nothing of the last four minutes.

"Put it on the list," Aurel says.

*It is not a route. Komárno is finished. Devín is finished. The gravel run belongs to them and the Brno cell is gone and I have nothing south of the river that I would put a passenger on.*

"Put it on the list."

*Aurel.* She lets it come out at the speed it wants to, which is the speed he can hear. *You are not putting it on the list because it is a witness. You are putting it on the list because it is your batch, and I want that said once, out loud, by me, at four in the morning, with nobody here but a boy who is not listening — because in three weeks you will have arranged it so that it was always an operational decision, and I will have helped you, and neither of us will remember that this is the moment.*

Aurel Steiner stands in the door of a sheep hut in the Hainburg hills, eighty-five years old, with his hands hanging, looking at a city he has never been to.

"Yes," he says. "Put it on the list."

She opens the Manifest.

**413 · —**

---

**END OF PART TWO**
